WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
A newborn excitatory cortical neuron has no legs.

2
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.800
It is born near the fluid -filled ventricle.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.060
Its layer lies near the outer surface, across a thick wall.

4
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.320
Later in development, it climbs a fiber of a radial glial cell.

5
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.740
Now imagine its front reaching forward while its nucleus stays behind.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.580
What pulls the nucleus?

7
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:30.380
Radial glee keep a long fiber running from the ventricle to the outer surface.

8
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.260
The wall is strung with parallel fibers.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.520
A neuron born beside the ventricle can climb such a fiber.

10
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:43.400
In culture, mouse neurons from the cerebellum were filmed moving along glial fibers.

11
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.960
They moved in a stop -and -go rhythm.

12
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.140
The tip of the cell extended and retracted out of step with the cell body.

13
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.363
The forward movement of the nucleus is called nucleokinesis.

14
00:00:54.823 --> 00:00:56.503
It runs as a two stroke cycle.

15
00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:59.443
In stroke one, the leading process extends.

16
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:02.503
A swelling forms in it, ahead of the nucleus.

17
00:01:03.023 --> 00:01:06.003
The centrosome and the Golgi move forward into the swelling.

18
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.713
In stroke two, the nucleus is pulled forward into the swelling.

19
00:01:11.273 --> 00:01:13.053
The trailing part of the cell follows.

20
00:01:13.713 --> 00:01:14.833
Then the cycle repeats.

21
00:01:15.653 --> 00:01:16.713
Think of an inchworm.

22
00:01:17.313 --> 00:01:18.733
The front reaches and grips.

23
00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:20.473
Then the back catches up.

24
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:22.347
What does the pulling?

25
00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:28.867
In newborn rat olfactory bulb precursors fixed mid -cycle, microtubules wrapped

26
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:30.667
around the nucleus like a cage.

27
00:01:31.207 --> 00:01:33.227
They ran forward toward the centrosome.

28
00:01:33.987 --> 00:01:35.267
That cage is the track.

29
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:39.767
Dineen is a motor that walks toward the minus ends of microtubules.

30
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.207
Those ends sit at the centrosome.

31
00:01:42.747 --> 00:01:46.807
Anchored around the nucleus, dineen is thought to draw the nucleus toward the

32
00:01:46.807 --> 00:01:50.087
centrosome. Myosin II adds a second force.

33
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:52.227
Where it acts is still argued.

34
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.960
Normally the nucleus follows the front.

35
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.420
Now break the pull.

36
00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.840
Knock down LIS -1 in embryonic rat cortex, and the cell body stops moving.

37
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.480
LIS -1 regulates dynein.

38
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.380
Losing one copy is enough to cause lissencephaly, a cortex with reduced folding.

39
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.760
Now block myosin -2 in newborn rat olfactory bulb precursors.

40
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.260
The leading process can keep growing while the cell body stops.

41
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.080
The front reaches first.

42
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.180
The nucleus is pulled after it.
